Q: Inferring types of optional properties A: <p>There is unfortunately no guarantee that the <code>TField1</code> or <code>TField2</code> generic type arguments will include <code>undefined</code> when the <code>field1</code> or <code>field2</code> fields of the constructor argument is <code>undefined</code>. You can guide <em>inference</em> to make that very likely, but it's not guaranteed. If you have a value of type <code>{x?: X}</code> then <code>x</code> can be <code>undefined</code> even if <code>X</code> is not, and that's what the compiler is complaining about.</p>
<p>For example, callers can always circumvent type argument inference by manually specifying type arguments themselves:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>const r = new ProcessedResult&lt;string, string&gt;({}); // allowed
//    ^? const r: ProcessedResult&lt;string, string&gt;;
r.field1.toUpperCase(); // TS accepts this but it's a guaranteed runtime error
</code></pre>
<p>That call is allowed because it's always allowed for callers to do that. So now <code>TField1</code> and <code>Tfield2</code> are <code>string</code>, and the <code>options</code> constructor parameter is <code>ProcessedResultOptions&lt;string, string&gt;</code>, which accepts <code>{}</code> because the properties are optional.  But now TypeScript thinks <code>r.field1</code> and <code>r.field2</code> are of type <code>string</code> when they are definitely <code>undefined</code></p>
<p>Another example, even with your <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-2-3.html#generic-parameter-defaults:%7E:text=Default%20types%20for%20a%20type%20parameter%20must%20satisfy%20the%20constraint%20for%20the%20type%20parameter%2C%20if%20it%20exists." rel="nofollow noreferrer">generic type argument defaults</a> of <code>undefined</code>, inference will sometimes still remove <code>undefined</code> if you pass in a <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/everyday-types.html#union-types" rel="nofollow noreferrer">union-typed</a> value like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>const r = new ProcessedResult({ field1: Math.random() &lt; 0.9999 ? undefined : &quot;abc&quot; });
//    ^? const r: ProcessedResult&lt;string, undefined&gt;
r.field1.toUpperCase(); // TS accepts this but it's 99.99%-guaranteed runtime error
</code></pre>
<p>Inference &quot;helpfully&quot; removes <code>undefined</code> from <code>TField1</code> in this case, since it pattern-matches <code>TField1 | undefined</code> against <code>string | undefined</code> and comes away with <code>string</code>.  And now again, you have a situation where TypeScript thinks <code>r.field1</code> is definitely a <code>string</code> but it is very likely to be <code>undefined</code> at runtime.</p>
<p>So that's the answer to your question as asked: TypeScript is warning you about a real, if unlikely, type safety hazard.</p>
<hr />
<p>The right way to proceed here depends on use cases. Obviously you can just use a <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/everyday-types.html#type-assertions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">type assertion</a> to allow the assignments inside the constructor. Your expected use case will still work as you intend, and the edge cases above might not happen enough for you to worry about them.</p>
<p>Ideally you'd want to tell TypeScript that it should use the default <em>value</em> of <code>undefined</code> to specify the type argument in the case where someone leaves out a field.  But that feature is not part of TypeScript. There's a very similar feature request at <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/58977" rel="nofollow noreferrer">microsoft/TypeScript#58977</a> to allow something like <code>function foo&lt;T&gt;(x: T = &quot;abc&quot;) {}</code> to work, where the default argument of <code>&quot;abc&quot;</code> would be used to specify <code>T</code>. Right now that fails for the same reason your code fails, because nothing actually guarantees that <code>&quot;abc&quot;</code> will be assignable to <code>T</code>.  And no, generic type argument defaults like <code>function foo&lt;T = string&gt;(x: T = &quot;abc&quot;)</code> don't help.</p>
<p>In the absence of the ideal approach, then, there are just workarounds.  One general class of workaround is to replace the <code>undefined</code> type with <code>unknown</code>, since TypeScript can't really be sure that a value of a type like <code>{field1: string}</code> actually <em>lacks</em> a <code>field2</code> field. The type of the <code>field2</code> property is therefore <code>unknown</code>, not <code>undefined</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>interface Hmm { field1: string };
const v = { field1: &quot;&quot;, field2: 3 };
const hmm: Hmm = v;
const r = new ProcessedResult(hmm); 
r.field2; // &lt;-- this is 3 at runtime, not undefined.
</code></pre>
<p>If so then you can make your class generic in the type of <code>options</code> and derive your old <code>TField1</code> and <code>TField2</code> from them via <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/indexed-access-types.html#handbook-content" rel="nofollow noreferrer">indexed access types</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>interface ProcessedResultOptions {
    field1?: unknown;
    field2?: unknown;
}

class ProcessedResult&lt;const T extends ProcessedResultOptions&gt; {
    public readonly field1: T[&quot;field1&quot;];
    public readonly field2: T[&quot;field2&quot;];
    constructor(options: T) { 
        this.field1 = options.field1;
        this.field2 = options.field2;
    }
}

const r = new ProcessedResult({field1: &quot;&quot;});
//    ^? const r: ProcessedResult&lt;{  readonly field1: &quot;&quot; }&gt;
r.field1.toUpperCase();
r.field2
//  ^? (property) ProcessedResult&lt;{ readonly field1: &quot;&quot;; }&gt;.field2: unknown
r.field2.toUpperCase(); // compiler error
</code></pre>
<p>This might not be the way you want to go, and it has other caveats, so I'm not going to go even farther out of scope to detail it. Personally I'd just use type assertions where appropriate and accept that some edge cases might break my assumptions.</p>
